ount', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\n7: 1, 6: 1, 5: 1, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\npair: ♥9 ♦9\nsingle: ♥5\nsingle: ♠8\nsingle: ♦9\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:\nLet me analyze the situation:\n- I have 4 cards: ♥5 ♠8 ♥9 ♦9\n- Landlord (opponent) has 3 cards\n- Teammate has 2 cards\n- This is a Free Play, so I must play something\n\nMy options:\n1. **Pair ♥9 ♦9**: Play my highest cards as a pair, leaving me with ♥5 ♠8 (2 cards)\n2. **Single ♥5**: Play my lowest card, leaving me with 3 cards\n3. **Single ♠8**: Play middle card, leaving me with 3 cards  \n4. **Single ♦9**: Play one high card, leaving me with 3 cards\n\nKey considerations:\n- My teammate has only 2 cards left and is very close to winning\n- The landlord has 3 cards\n- I should help my teammate win by giving them opportunities to play out\n- From card counting: 7:1, 6:1, 5:1, 3:2 remain unknown\n- My teammate likely needs to play their last 2 cards soon\n\nStrategy: Since my teammate has only 2 cards, they might have a pair or be waiting for the right moment. If I play the pair ♥9 ♦9, I\'ll have 2 cards left (♥5 ♠8), which puts pressure on the landlord. More importantly, after I play, my teammate gets a chance to respond. If they can beat my pair or I play low enough singles later, they might win.\n\nHowever, playing a low single like ♥5 gives my teammate more flexibility to play over it with potentially anything, and keeps my pair intact as a strong combination for later.\n\nActually, the best move is to play **pair ♥9 ♦9** because:\n- It reduces my hand to 2 cards (matching teammate\'s count)\n- It\'s harder for the landlord to beat a pair than a single\n- It gives my teammate a chance to respond\n\nReason:\nPlaying the pair ♥9 ♦9 is optimal because it reduces my hand to just 2 cards, matching my teammate\'s card count. This puts maximum pressure on the landlord who has 3 cards. A pair is also more restrictive than a single - the landlord must respond with a higher pair or pass, which limits their options. This gives my teammate the best chance to either beat this play or get control when the landlord passes.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥9", "♦9"]}'}
